#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199027} :activity/id #uuid "bd6ad418-e22b-5009-b330-e1172a694c56" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050536 :topic/id #uuid "51d6809f-cbef-5f23-be22-3c2d79930c37" :topic/math-academy-id 4218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199508 :tutorial/content "We can use differentiation to find the shortest distance between a point and a curve. \n\nFor example, let's find the smallest distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1$ and the point $(5,1),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1f5b350025a0fd0530531cfc887c148459194afe209e472701054c128f1079.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1.** Use the distance formula to create a function $S(x,y)$ representing the *squared* distance between $(5,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\nS = (x-5)^2 + (y-1)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Calculate the first derivative $S'(x)$ and solve $S'(x) = 0$ to find the stationary point(s).\n\nComputing the derivative $S'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(x) & =2x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where $S'(x) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-8 & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Use the first or second derivative tests to confirm that the stationary point minimizes the squared distance.\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\nS''(4) = 2.\n$$\n\nSince $S''(4) > 0,$ the point $x=4$ is a minimum of $S(x)$ by the second derivative test.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x = 4.$ \n\nFinally, since $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ef45dd95-3bca-5734-a692-3cebc9f37432" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7893 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25198a50-d410-5851-a1cc-00f60435537f" :step/math-academy-id 119264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198079 :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050952}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403011}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"} :step/id #uuid "e07168c9-fa86-5a07-b2f0-b9f7aade6c3b" :step/math-academy-id 119265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199507 :tutorial/content "If the squared distance function has multiple stationary points, we must use the candidates test for *global* extrema to identify which stationary point corresponds to the smallest distance.\n\nTo do that, we evaluate the squared distance at each stationary point and compare the results.\n\nMultiple critical points may correspond to the same squared distance. In such cases, like that shown in the graph below, there would be multiple points of minimum distance.\n\n![](images/de/dea83b2804a816eef505810237940e2d9bb8547ffe77549f833ac04823fa6d30.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c98fb0ba-d8a2-5069-b3c5-04961452caa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7889 :tutorial/title "The Candidates Test For Multiple Stationary Points"} :step/id #uuid "eff95439-12b9-5276-a8f1-b3b4ee7e96f1" :step/math-academy-id 119268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198080 :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317549}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"} :step/id #uuid "97876c5f-6760-51ef-a427-274559dccac4" :step/math-academy-id 119269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198083 :question/id #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" :question/math-academy-id "e-12606"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317556}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "798c8ee2-b4c9-57b8-b340-831e2641810f" :step/math-academy-id 119272}] :activity/title "Optimizing Distances to Curves" :activity/type {:db/i